    "summary": "First New Speedway Boogie debut with solid jams despite rough tape",
    "blurb": "The show delivers the historic first performance of New Speedway Boogie, a tight China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider segue, and an exploratory The Eleven. Recording is a fair‑quality soundboard with noticeable hiss, occasional skips and dropouts, making it uneven but essential for collectors.",
    "review": "December 20, 1969 at the Fillmore captures a pivotal moment in the Dead’s evolution – the debut of New Speedway Boogie. The band launches into an early‑era groove that hints at the song’s future live life, while Phil Lesh adds a loose \"Feelin' Groovy\" vibe during Dark Star that helps steer the improvisations forward. Highlights include a crisp China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider transition and an adventurous run through The Eleven, both showcasing the group’s growing telepathic interplay. The second‑set finale, Turn On Your Lovelight, features Pigpen’s raucous rap, adding raw energy despite the tape’s hiss and occasional skips. Though the soundboard capture is far from pristine, its historical significance and strong performances make it a worthwhile listen for Deadheads seeking this unique snapshot of the band in transition.",
